  "narrative": {
    "intro": "McKesson obligated $11.1B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date, across 2,201 awards with an average award size of $5M. Obligations are down 6.7% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations rose from $7.7B in FY21 to $8.4B in FY22 and $9.5B in FY23, then moved to $9B in FY24 and $11.9B in FY25 before reaching $11.1B in FY26 to date. The Department of Veterans Affairs is the largest buyer, having obligated $10.8B so far this fiscal year. The Department of Justice and Department of Health and Human Services follow at much smaller levels, with obligations of $168M and $88.1M respectively. Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing is the dominant NAICS market for McKesson, with obligations of $11.1B in FY2026 to date. Other listed markets include Drugs and Druggists' Sundries Merchant Wholesalers at $6.3M, Major Household Appliance Manufacturing at $37.5K, Medical Laboratories at $15K, Medicinal and Botanical Manufacturing at $14.6K, and General Medical and Surgical Hospitals at $259. The Department of Homeland Security obligated $9.3M and the Peace Corps obligated $405K in FY2026 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has McKesson's federal business changed recently?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 6.7% year over year. McKesson obligated $11.1B in FY2026 to date, after $11.9B in FY25 and $9B in FY24. The multi-year series shows growth from $7.7B in FY21 to $9.5B in FY23 before recent fluctuations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who buys the most from McKesson in the federal government?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs is the largest buyer, having obligated $10.8B in FY2026 to date. The Department of Justice obligated $168M and the Department of Health and Human Services obligated $88.1M, with the Department of Homeland Security at $9.3M and the Peace Corps at $405K."
      }
    ]
  },
